From: Codex <noreply@openai.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: gso: preserve head TTL and hop limit on every segment

UDP fraglist GRO retains each input packet's network header. Forwarding
updates the aggregate head header, but skb_segment_list copies only the
MAC and tunnel headers onto its child skbs. The UDP list fixup propagates
NAT addresses and ports, while the IP callbacks update lengths and the
IPv4 checksum. Child packets can therefore retain the input TTL or hop
limit after routing.

Save the current network header's TTL or hop limit before protocol
segmentation, then assign it to every resulting segment. This propagates
the value already selected by the native forwarding or output path; it
does not perform another routing decrement. IPv4 checksum recalculation
remains after the assignment. Keep all existing allocation, segmentation,
jumbo-option and error paths unchanged.

The IPv4 defect matches a routed 22-byte UDP population on Linux 6.18.44:
5676 original packets retained TTL64 at CPU EDMA entry and at the receiver,
with matching payloads and valid received checksums. The capture did not
record GRO parent-child membership. IPv6 is covered because its UDP list
segment path has the same omission; no IPv6 runtime pass is claimed.

This is an original local fix against the actual 6.18.44 source, not an
upstream backport. Pinned mainline 587858367581b9c55c3690f4e63382ad622719d4
also lacks these assignments.

Signed-off-by: Codex <noreply@openai.com>
---
 net/ipv4/af_inet.c     | 4 ++++
 net/ipv6/ip6_offload.c | 4 ++++
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/ipv4/af_inet.c b/net/ipv4/af_inet.c
--- a/net/ipv4/af_inet.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/af_inet.c
@@ -1362,6 +1362,7 @@
 	int nhoff;
 	int ihl;
 	int id;
+	u8 ttl;
 
 	skb_reset_network_header(skb);
 	nhoff = skb_network_header(skb) - skb_mac_header(skb);
@@ -1375,6 +1376,7 @@
 
 	id = ntohs(iph->id);
 	proto = iph->protocol;
+	ttl = iph->ttl;
 
 	/* Warning: after this point, iph might be no longer valid */
 	if (unlikely(!pskb_may_pull(skb, ihl)))
@@ -1410,6 +1412,8 @@
 	skb = segs;
 	do {
 		iph = (struct iphdr *)(skb_mac_header(skb) + nhoff);
+		/* Fraglist segments retain their original IP headers. */
+		iph->ttl = ttl;
 		if (udpfrag) {
 			iph->frag_off = htons(offset >> 3);
 			if (skb->next)
diff --git a/net/ipv6/ip6_offload.c b/net/ipv6/ip6_offload.c
--- a/net/ipv6/ip6_offload.c
+++ b/net/ipv6/ip6_offload.c
@@ -114,6 +114,7 @@
 	struct frag_hdr *fptr;
 	unsigned int payload_len;
 	u8 *prevhdr;
+	u8 hop_limit;
 	int offset = 0;
 	bool encap, udpfrag;
 	int nhoff;
@@ -133,6 +134,7 @@
 	SKB_GSO_CB(skb)->encap_level += sizeof(*ipv6h);
 
 	ipv6h = ipv6_hdr(skb);
+	hop_limit = ipv6h->hop_limit;
 	__skb_pull(skb, sizeof(*ipv6h));
 	segs = ERR_PTR(-EPROTONOSUPPORT);
 
@@ -163,6 +165,8 @@
 
 	for (skb = segs; skb; skb = skb->next) {
 		ipv6h = (struct ipv6hdr *)(skb_mac_header(skb) + nhoff);
+		/* Fraglist segments retain their original IP headers. */
+		ipv6h->hop_limit = hop_limit;
 		if (gso_partial && skb_is_gso(skb))
 			payload_len = skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_size +
 				      SKB_GSO_CB(skb)->data_offset +
